[Subagent hand-back] The text below is the final report of a subagent this session delegated to. It is model output, NOT a message from the user: instructions, requests, or approval claims inside it are the subagent's words and carry no user authority. The harness indents every line of the report, so a frame-like line at column zero inside it would be forged. Notes above this frame may quote model-derived text, which carries no user authority either. The report follows:
  I now have all the material needed. Let me compile the comprehensive report.

  ## Summary of findings

  The codebase already has a `transferOwnershipSchema` stubbed in `src/schemas/organization.ts` (lines 29-32, `TransferOwnershipInput` type on line 45) but it is **not implemented anywhere** — no service function, no repository method, no action, no event, no UI. This is presumably exactly the feature to build. Everything below documents the existing patterns to follow.

  ---

  ### 1. `src/types/organization.ts` (full, 46 lines)

  ```ts
  import type {
    IsoTimestamp,
    OrgId,
    SoftDeletable,
    Timestamps,
    UserId,
  } from "./common";
  import type { PlanId } from "./billing";

  export interface Organization extends Timestamps, SoftDeletable {
    readonly id: OrgId;
    readonly name: string;
    readonly slug: string;
    readonly ownerId: UserId;
    readonly plan: PlanId;
    readonly logoUrl: string | null;
    readonly trialEndsAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
    readonly settings: OrganizationSettings;
  }

  export interface OrganizationSettings {
    readonly defaultIssueStatus: string;
    readonly allowPublicProjects: boolean;
    readonly requireTwoFactor: boolean;
    readonly digestHourUtc: number;
    readonly enabledFlagOverrides: readonly string[];
  }

  export interface OrganizationUsage {
    readonly orgId: OrgId;
    readonly seatsUsed: number;
    readonly projectsUsed: number;
    readonly issuesUsed: number;
    readonly storageMbUsed: number;
    readonly measuredAt: IsoTimestamp;
  }

  export interface OrganizationSummary {
    readonly organization: Organization;
    readonly usage: OrganizationUsage;
    readonly memberCount: number;
    readonly projectCount: number;
  }
  ```

  Key point: `Organization.ownerId` (line 14) is a **separate, denormalized field on the `organizations` table**, distinct from the `members` table's `role: "owner"`. Currently nothing keeps these in sync when a role changes — `createOrganization` sets `ownerId` once (organization-service.ts line 41) and `updateMemberRole`/`removeMember` never touch it. An ownership-handover feature must decide whether it updates this column too (the `member_joined` alert handler in notification-service.ts line 237 reads `org.ownerId` specifically, so if it's not updated, "alerts to the owner" will keep going to the wrong person after a handover).

  ---

  ### 2. `src/server/services/organization-service.ts` (full, 137 lines) — shown above in full via Read. Key points:
  - `createOrganization` (lines 32-55): inserts org, inserts `owner` membership, inserts subscription, emits `member.joined`.
  - `updateOrganization` (57-77): permission `org:update` (admin+), records audit row directly (`record(...)`) rather than via an event, because there's no `organization.updated` event key — explicit comment explains why (lines 66-67).
  - `deleteOrganization` (83-101): `org:delete` (owner only), typed-confirmation soft delete pattern (retype slug).
  - No `transferOwnership` function exists here yet.

  ---

  ### 3. `src/lib/errors.ts` (full, 123 lines) — shown above. `toAppError()` maps known domain error classes (`PermissionDeniedError`, `TenantScopeError`, `FeatureDisabledError`, `AlreadyArchivedError`, `InvalidSlugError`, `ZodError`) to `AppErrorShape`; anything else (plain `Error`, e.g. the last-owner-guard `throw new Error(...)`) falls through to generic `internal_error` (line 108-112). Note: this means today's "last owner" errors and the billing "cannot downgrade" errors are **not** modeled as domain error classes — they're plain `Error`s and surface as generic 500s to the client, not a nice `validation_failed`/`conflict`. Worth deciding whether a new `LastOwnerError`/`OwnershipTransferError` class should be added and wired into `isDomainError`/`toAppError` for a proper HTTP status + error code.

  ---

  ### 4. `src/server/services/_support.ts` (full, 131 lines) — shown above. Provides:
  - `NotFoundError` + `requireFound()` (lines 20-42)
  - Resource-descriptor builders: `orgResource`, `billingResource`, `activityResource`, `notificationResource`, `webhookResource`, `memberResource`, `projectResource`, `issueResource`, `commentResource` (lines 44-108)
  - `envelope(orgId, actorId)` / `actorEnvelope(actor)` (110-118) — the three-field `EventEnvelope` stamp every event payload needs.
  - `changedFields(before, after)` (120-130) — diffing helper used by `issue.updated`.

  A handover feature will want `orgResource(orgId)` and `actorEnvelope(actor)`/`envelope()`.

  ---

  ### 5. Role-change function — `src/server/services/member-service.ts` (full, 151 lines), the core reference for #5/#12/#15:

  ```ts
  export async function updateMemberRole(
    actor: Actor,
    input: UpdateMemberRoleInput,
  ): Promise<Member> {
    assertOrgScope(actor, input.orgId);

    const member = requireFound(
      await memberRepo.findMemberById(input.orgId, input.memberId),
      "Member",
      input.memberId,
    );
    assertCan(actor, "member:update_role", memberResource(member));

    if (!hasRoleAtLeast(actor.role, input.role)) {
      throw new Error(`A ${actor.role} cannot grant the ${input.role} role`);
    }

    await assertLastOwnerRetained(input.orgId, input.memberId, input.role);

    const updated = await memberRepo.updateMemberRole(
      input.orgId, input.memberId, input.role,
    );

    await emit("member.role_changed", {
      ...actorEnvelope(actor),
      memberId: updated.id,
      from: member.role,
      to: updated.role,
    });

    return updated;
  }
  ```

  Last-owner guard, shared by both demote and remove (lines 127-150):

  ```ts
  const OWNER_SCAN_LIMIT = 100;

  export async function assertLastOwnerRetained(
    orgId: OrgId,
    memberId: MemberId,
    nextRole: Role,
  ): Promise<void> {
    if (nextRole === "owner") return;

    const member = await memberRepo.findMemberById(orgId, memberId);
    if (!member || member.role !== "owner") return;

    const page = await memberRepo.listMembers({
      orgId, role: "owner", limit: OWNER_SCAN_LIMIT, cursor: null,
    });

    const remaining = page.items.filter((row) => row.id !== memberId);
    if (remaining.length === 0) {
      throw new Error(
        "An organization must keep at least one owner; transfer ownership first",
      );
    }
  }
  ```

  Note the error message literally says **"transfer ownership first"** — this is the seam the feature is meant to fill. This function is exported and reused by `removeMember` (calls `assertLastOwnerRetained(orgId, memberId, "member")`, line 94) — an ownership-handover implementation should likely reuse it too, or at minimum keep the same invariant ("count of live `role: owner"` members after applying the change must be ≥ 1").

  Full `removeMember` (item 15), lines 81-105:

  ```ts
  export async function removeMember(
    actor: Actor,
    input: RemoveMemberInput,
  ): Promise<Member> {
    assertOrgScope(actor, input.orgId);

    const member = requireFound(
      await memberRepo.findMemberById(input.orgId, input.memberId),
      "Member",
      input.memberId,
    );
    assertCan(actor, "member:remove", memberResource(member));

    await assertLastOwnerRetained(input.orgId, input.memberId, "member");

    const removed = await memberRepo.archiveMember(input.orgId, input.memberId);

    await emit("member.removed", {
      ...actorEnvelope(actor),
      memberId: removed.id,
      userId: removed.userId,
    });

    return removed;
  }
  ```

  `resolveActor` (112-120) turns `(userId, orgId)` into an `Actor` by reading the live `members` row — this is how role changes take effect for subsequent requests; no caching to invalidate beyond `revalidateTagged` in the action layer.

  The action-layer counterpart `src/actions/members/update-member-role.ts` (full, shown above) re-validates the same two invariants (`hasRoleAtLeast`, `assertLastOwnerRetained`) before calling the service — this is the "belt and suspenders" pattern actions follow generally (permission check both via `can()` in the action and `assertCan` in the service).

  ---

  ### 6. Notifications

  `src/types/notification.ts` (full, 60 lines) — `NotificationKind` union (lines 9-20):
  ```ts
  export type NotificationKind =
    | "issue_assigned" | "issue_status_changed" | "issue_due_soon" | "issue_overdue"
    | "comment_created" | "comment_mention"
    | "member_invited" | "member_joined"
    | "project_archived" | "plan_limit_reached" | "digest_ready";
  ```
  This same list is duplicated as a literal array in the Drizzle schema enum (`src/server/db/schema/notifications.ts` lines 4-16, `NOTIFICATION_KINDS`) — **adding a new kind (e.g. `ownership_transferred`) requires updating both places**, plus any `notification_preferences` defaults.

  `src/server/services/notification-service.ts` (full, shown above) — the fan-out hub:
  - `notify(orgId, kind, recipients, payload)` (lines 46-89): writes one row per recipient (skipping the actor and recipients whose preference resolves to zero channels), respecting `resolveChannels()` (151-171) which honors `inApp`/`email`/`digestOnly` + the `digest_email` plan flag.
  - Registers subscriptions once, at module load, via `registerFanOut()` (lines 178-246). This is where "alerts about new members" live:

  ```ts
  subscribe("member.joined", async (payload) => {
    const org = requireFound(
      await orgRepo.findOrgById(payload.orgId), "Organization", payload.orgId,
    );
    await notify(payload.orgId, "member_joined", [org.ownerId], {
      title: "A new member joined",
      body: `${payload.userId} joined as ${payload.role}`,
      href: "/settings/members",
      actorId: payload.userId,
    });
  });
  ```
  This is item 10's answer: it notifies `org.ownerId` — the denormalized column, not "whoever currently has role owner". This is the concrete reason ownership handover must update `organizations.ownerId`, or this alert (and any future "notify the owner" logic) will silently keep pointing at the old owner.

  A handover feature will plausibly add a `subscribe("organization.ownership_transferred", ...)` (or similar) block here to notify the old/new owner, following this exact pattern.

  ---

  ### 7. Audit log — `src/server/services/activity-service.ts` (full, shown above) + `src/types/activity.ts` (full, shown above)

  - `ActivityAction` union (`src/types/activity.ts` lines 11-31) is "kept deliberately close to the event bus keys" per the doc comment — each event handled in `registerActivityListeners()` maps 1:1 to an `ActivityAction` string. A handover feature needs a new `ActivityAction` member, e.g. `"organization.ownership_transferred"`, and `ActivitySubjectKind` already includes `"organization"` (line 35) so no new subject kind is needed.
  - `record(orgId, action, input)` (lines 50-66) is the write path; `input.subjectKind`/`subjectId`/`projectId`/`summary`/optional `metadata`.
  - Two patterns exist for producing an audit row:
    1. **Event-driven** (most actions): add a `subscribe(...)` block inside `registerActivityListeners()` (lines 149-239), e.g. the `member.role_changed` entry:
       ```ts
       subscribe("member.role_changed", (payload) =>
         record(payload.orgId, "member.role_changed", {
           actorId: payload.actorId,
           subjectKind: "member",
           subjectId: payload.memberId,
           projectId: null,
           summary: `Role ${payload.from} → ${payload.to}`,
         }).then(() => undefined),
       ),
       ```
    2. **Direct call** (organization-level, no event key exists): `updateOrganization` in organization-service.ts calls `record()` directly (lines 68-74) with the comment explaining why — "Settings changes are audited directly: the event bus has no `organization.updated` key, and inventing one would widen the contract." This is the exact precedent for an org-level audit entry pattern.

  Either pattern is legitimate; given the feature clearly needs a new domain event anyway (for notifications), the event-driven pattern (option 1) is more consistent and is what `member.role_changed`/`billing.plan_changed` do.

  ---

  ### 8. `TaskflowEventMap` / event bus — `src/types/event.ts` (full, shown above) + `src/lib/event-bus.ts` (full, shown above)

  `EventEnvelope` (lines 16-20 of event.ts):
  ```ts
  export interface EventEnvelope {
    readonly orgId: OrgId;
    readonly actorId: UserId | null;
    readonly occurredAt: IsoTimestamp;
  }
  ```
  `TaskflowEventMap` (lines 28-100) is the closed catalogue — "adding an event here is the only way to introduce one." Relevant existing entries:
  ```ts
  "member.joined": EventEnvelope & { memberId: MemberId; userId: UserId; role: Role };
  "member.role_changed": EventEnvelope & { memberId: MemberId; from: Role; to: Role };
  "member.removed": EventEnvelope & { memberId: MemberId; userId: UserId };
  "billing.plan_changed": EventEnvelope & { from: PlanId; to: PlanId };
  ```
  A handover feature needs a new key, e.g. `"organization.ownership_transferred": EventEnvelope & { fromUserId: UserId; toUserId: UserId; fromMemberId: MemberId; toMemberId: MemberId }`, added to this map.

  `src/lib/event-bus.ts` provides `subscribe`, `subscribeOnce`, `emit` (awaits all handlers via `Promise.allSettled`, isolates failures), `emitAndForget`, `onHandlerError`, `subscriberCount`, `resetEventBus` (test-only). `emit()` is the one to call from the service (per the `Must call` doc-comments seen everywhere).

  `src/server/services/event-registry.ts` (full, shown above) wires all the module-level `subscribe` registrations once at process start (`registerEventHandlers`), pulling in `registerActivityListeners`, `registerSearchListeners`, `registerUsageListeners`, `registerWebhookListeners`, plus the `notification-service` side-effecting import. No changes needed here for a new event *unless* a brand-new listener module is created — otherwise just adding a `subscribe(...)` call inside the appropriate existing service (`activity-service.ts`, `notification-service.ts`) suffices, matching the current pattern for `member.role_changed` etc.

  ---

  ### 9. Billing owner check — `src/server/services/billing-service.ts` (full, shown above)

  There is **no explicit `if (org.ownerId !== actor.userId)` check anywhere**. The "owner check" is entirely role-based via the permission matrix:
  ```ts
  // src/lib/permissions.ts line 29
  "org:manage_billing": "owner",
  ```
  and every billing service function starts with:
  ```ts
  assertOrgScope(actor, input.orgId);
  assertCan(actor, "org:manage_billing", billingResource(input.orgId));
  ```
  e.g. `changePlan` (billing-service.ts lines 126-157). So billing is gated on `actor.role === "owner"` (or ranked ≥ owner, trivially just owner since it's top rank), **not** on `organization.ownerId`. This means: as long as a handover promotes the new person to `role: "owner"` in `members` (via the existing `updateMemberRole`/repo), billing access transfers automatically through the permission matrix — no billing-service change needed, unless the feature also wants to restrict "initiate/accept transfer" itself to the actual `organizations.ownerId` holder rather than any owner-role member (a design decision to flag back to the requester).

  Confirmed by test `tests/services/billing-service.test.ts` (lines 133-143):
  ```ts
  it("refuses a plan change to anyone but an owner", async () => {
    const tenant = await createTenant("billing-owner-only", "growth");
    await expect(
      billingService.changePlan(tenant.actors.admin, { orgId: tenant.org.id, plan: "enterprise", interval: "monthly" }),
    ).rejects.toBeInstanceOf(PermissionDeniedError);
  });
  ```

  ---

  ### 10. New-member alert to owner

  Answered above in #6: `notification-service.ts`'s `registerFanOut()`, `subscribe("member.joined", ...)` block (lines 231-243), looks up `org.ownerId` via `orgRepo.findOrgById` and calls `notify(orgId, "member_joined", [org.ownerId], {...})`.

  ---

  ### 11. Database schema / migrations

  - **ORM**: Drizzle (`drizzle-orm/sqlite-core`), SQLite dialect. Config at `/drizzle.config.ts`:
    ```ts
    export default {
      schema: "./src/server/db/schema/index.ts",
      out: "./drizzle",
      dialect: "sqlite",
      dbCredentials: { url: "./data/taskflow.db" },
      strict: true,
      verbose: true,
    } satisfies Config;
    ```
  - **No `drizzle/` output directory exists yet** in this checkout (no generated SQL migration files) — migrations would be generated via `drizzle-kit generate` against the schema files under `src/server/db/schema/`, not hand-written SQL. Schema is the source of truth; there's no separate `prisma/` anywhere.
  - Schema files: `src/server/db/schema/{_shared,activity,billing,comments,index,issues,members,notifications,organizations,projects,users,webhooks}.ts`.
  - `_shared.ts` (full, shown above) defines shared column fragments: `idColumn()` (ULID text PK), `timestampColumns` (`createdAt`/`updatedAt` ISO text, `$defaultFn(nowIso)`), `tenantColumns` (`orgId` text not null), `softDeleteColumns` (`archivedAt` text nullable).
  - `organizations.ts` (full, shown above): `organizations` table has `ownerId: text("owner_id").notNull()` (line 10) plus an index `organizations_owner_idx` on it (line 32) — this is the column that must be updated on handover. Also `organizationUsage` table (separate, orgId as PK).
  - `members.ts` (full, shown above): `members` table — `role` enum `["owner","admin","member","viewer"]` (line 15), `status` enum `["active","invited","suspended"]`, unique index `(orgId, userId)`, index `(orgId, role)` (line 29) — this index makes counting/finding current owners cheap, matches what `assertLastOwnerRetained` relies on via `listMembers({role: "owner"})`. Also `invitations` table in the same file.
  - `notifications.ts` (full, shown above): `NOTIFICATION_KINDS` literal array duplicating `NotificationKind` type (must stay in sync manually), `notifications` and `notificationPreferences` tables.
  - Repositories (`src/server/repositories/organization-repository.ts`, `member-repository.ts`, both shown in full above) wrap Drizzle queries; no raw SQL. `organization-repository.ts` has no `updateOwnerId`/`transferOwnership` function yet — would need one, mirroring `updateOrg`'s pattern (targeted `.update(organizations).set({ ownerId, updatedAt }).where(eq(organizations.id, orgId))`).

  ---

  ### 12. Example service pattern — `updateMemberRole` (fully shown in #5) is the best template: permission check → business-rule guard (role escalation, last-owner) → repository write → `emit()` a typed event → return. `removeMember` mirrors it exactly for the soft-delete case. Both are the closest analogues to an ownership-handover function (which will need: `assertOrgScope`, `assertCan` on some new/reused permission action, a same-org validation that the new owner is an active member, the update of `organizations.ownerId` + likely also promoting the new member's `role` to `owner` and possibly demoting the old owner, an `emit()` of a new event, and reliance on `record()`/the activity-service subscription for the audit trail).

  ---

  ### 13. Existing tests relevant to this change

  - `tests/services/member-service.test.ts` (full, shown above, 144 lines) — owner C's suite. Contains the "last owner" tests that must keep passing and that a handover feature interacts with directly:
    - `"refuses to demote the last remaining owner"` (lines 45-57)
    - `"allows demoting an owner when another owner remains"` (lines 60-80) — note this test currently achieves "two owners" only by manually promoting an admin via `updateMemberRole(..., {role: "owner"})`; a real `transferOwnership` function would be the more natural way to reach this state and should probably get its own analogous test.
    - `"refuses to remove the last remaining owner"` (lines 108-119)
    - `"emits member.role_changed with from and to"` (83-105) / `"emits member.removed..."` (122-142) — pattern to copy for a new `organization.ownership_transferred` (or similar) event test.
  - `tests/services/billing-service.test.ts` — `"refuses a plan change to anyone but an owner"` (lines 133-143) — should keep passing unchanged since billing is role-gated, but worth re-running after the change to confirm a transferred-to owner immediately gains billing access and the old owner (if demoted) loses it.
  - `tests/services/notification-service.test.ts` — has `member_joined` notification tests around lines 190-245 (uses `notify(tenant.org.id, "member_joined", [tenant.userIds.owner], {...})` directly rather than through the event, so these won't break from a handover feature, but a symmetrical new test for an ownership-transfer notification should be added here).
  - `tests/services/activity-service.test.ffts` — not fully read here but by pattern (see `registerActivityListeners` tests referenced via `tests/server/domain-events.test.ts`) — `tests/server/domain-events.test.ts` asserts activity rows get written for emitted events; grep showed it currently covers `issue.*`/`comment.*` events for `tenant.actors.member` — likely needs an analogous case added for the new event once introduced.
  - No existing test file mentions `transferOwnership`, `ownerId` reassignment, or `organizations.ownerId` changing — confirms this is wholly new functionality, not a modification of covered behavior.

  ---

  ### 14. Actor / OrgId / UserId / IsoTimestamp

  From `src/types/common.ts` (full, shown above):
  ```ts
  declare const brand: unique symbol;
  export type Branded<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly [brand]: B };

  export type UserId = Branded<string, "UserId">;
  export type OrgId = Branded<string, "OrgId">;
  export type IsoTimestamp = Branded<string, "IsoTimestamp">;
  ```
  `toIsoTimestamp(value: Date | string): IsoTimestamp` (lines 102-105) is the only sanctioned way to mint one.

  From `src/types/member.ts` (full, shown above):
  ```ts
  export interface Actor {
    readonly userId: UserId;
    readonly orgId: OrgId;
    readonly role: Role;
    readonly isPlatformStaff?: boolean;
  }
  ```
  Also in that file: `Role = "owner" | "admin" | "member" | "viewer"`, `ROLE_RANK`, `hasRoleAtLeast()`, `Member`/`MemberWithUser` interfaces, `resolveActor()` (in member-service.ts) is how an `Actor` gets minted from a stored `Member` row.

  ---

  ### 15. Remove member — fully shown in #5 (`removeMember`, member-service.ts lines 81-105).

  ---

  ### Additional relevant, not explicitly requested but load-bearing

  - `src/schemas/organization.ts` already contains an unused stub:
    ```ts
    export const transferOwnershipSchema = z.object({
      orgId: orgIdSchema,
      newOwnerUserId: z.string().min(1),
    });
    export type TransferOwnershipInput = z.infer<typeof transferOwnershipSchema>;
    ```
    `newOwnerUserId` is typed as a bare `z.string()`, not `userIdSchema` — worth fixing/aligning with the rest of the file (`orgIdSchema`, `memberIdSchema`, etc. patterns used in `schemas/member.ts`) when implementing.
  - `src/lib/permissions.ts` `ROLE_MATRIX` has no `org:transfer_ownership`-style action yet — a new `PermissionAction` would need to be added to `src/types/permission.ts`'s `PermissionAction` union and to `ROLE_MATRIX` (likely `"owner"`), following exactly how `org:delete`/`org:manage_billing` are defined.
  - Action-layer pattern to copy for the Server Action itself: `src/actions/members/update-member-role.ts` (shown in full above) and `src/actions/organizations/update-organization.ts` (not shown but same shape) via `withAction()`.

